    "The second floor smells like paper, carpet cleaner, and a dozen people trying not to make noise while their lives quietly organize themselves."
    "Study tables line the windows."
    "Sunlight lands across them in pale rectangles, soft enough to make textbooks look briefly innocent."
    "Monika is already there."
    show monika 1a at t11
    "Of course she is already there."
    "She has claimed a table near the windows, arranged two chairs, stacked three review folders by subject, and color-coded a row of sticky notes with quiet conviction."
    "Her hair ribbon is tied neatly."
    "Her eyes look less neat."
    m "You made it."
    mc "I did promise."
    m "You also promised to sleep at a reasonable time."
    mc "I slept. The hour is between me and my alarm."
    "She lets that one go, which is how I know she is already tired."
    m "Sit."
    "I sit."
    "My bag lands beside the chair with the soft, heavy sound of the weekend giving up on being light."
    m "I didn't make us a minute-by-minute schedule, before you ask."
    mc "That's almost humane."
    m "Topic rotation. Civics and language arts first, then science, then algebra, then English."
    m "Short blocks. Breaks when one of us starts sounding less like a person."
    mc "Reasonable."
    "She squares her stack of sticky notes against the edge of the table, then opens the civics folder."
    "The Sunday shape settles around us."
    "Paper, pens, sunlight, the window table."
    "Her hand close enough to pass me a card without either of us needing to stand."
    m "Okay."
    m "Warmup question."
    m "When a court strikes down a law because it conflicts with the constitution, what principle is being applied?"
    mc "Judicial review."
    m "Definition?"
    mc "A court checks a law against the constitution and says the regular law loses if they conflict."
    m "Less casual."
    mc "The judiciary reviews government action for constitutional compatibility."
    m "Better."
    "She flips the card."
    m "Federalism."
    mc "Power split between levels of government."
    m "Example."
    mc "Local and national governments both exercising powers in their own spheres."
    m "Good."
    m "Due process."
    mc "Fair procedures before the government deprives someone of liberty or property."
    m "Why does that matter?"
    mc "Because authority isn't supposed to skip the process just because it wants an outcome."
    "Monika's pen stops."
    "Only for a fraction of a second."
    m "That is a strong answer."
    mc "That is a lot of sincere praise for nine in the morning."
    m "You can accept it."
    mc "I'm working on it. Might take a minute."
    "She writes something in the margin of her notes."
    m "Noted."
    "I lean back before the chair can creak too loudly."
    "A librarian passes behind us with a cart of returned books."
    "The wheels squeak once, then stop as if scolded by the room."
    "Monika moves the civics pile aside and slides over the language arts handout."
    m "Device, pressure, meaning."
    mc "I remember. Against my will, but I remember."
    m "Then answer. A narrator describes concrete actions but repeatedly omits the actor responsible. What is the effect?"
    mc "It forces the reader to participate in assigning agency."
    m "And?"
    mc "The action stays visible, but responsibility gets unstable."
    mc "The reader has to decide whether the narrator is uncertain, evasive, or redirecting blame."
    "Monika taps the page once."
    m "That is exactly what the exam wants."
    "I write reader assigns agency in the corner even though I already know it."
    "Writing it again feels like putting a nail into a loose board."
    m "Unreliable narrator."
    mc "A narrator whose account conflicts with evidence in the story."
    m "Not just first person."
    mc "Pronouns alone don't convict anybody."
    m "That sounds like your mother."
    "She means it kindly, so I let it stand instead of swatting it away."
    m "Recurring image supporting a theme."
    mc "A motif has to do actual work. Like a locked door that shows up whenever someone avoids a choice."
    mc "If the curtain is just blue one time because the room needed curtains, the curtain is unemployed."
    "Monika's laugh is quiet enough for the library."
    m "Please don't write unemployed curtain on the exam."
    mc "No promises."
    "The first block goes by faster than I expect."
    "That is probably Monika's fault."
    "She does not drill like a machine."
    "She asks, corrects, lets the answer land, then adds one little hook to keep it in place."
    "Judicial review becomes a court checking the higher rulebook."
    "Federalism becomes layers."
    "Due process becomes procedure before power."
    "Omitted agency becomes the reader being made to touch the sentence."
    "The notes stop looking like disconnected traps and start looking like things a person could walk through without stepping directly into every pit."
    "{i}Dangerous. I may be learning.{/i}"
    m "Break?"
    mc "Already?"
    m "We have been going for forty minutes."
    mc "Lies."
    m "The clock isn't collaborating with me for drama."
    "I look."
    "The clock, traitor that it is, proves her right."
    mc "Huh."
    m "Water."
    mc "President order?"
    m "Friend order."
    "That settles differently."
    "Not harder, just lower."
    mc "...Yeah."
    "I take the bottle from my bag and drink."
    "Monika looks out the window while I do, giving me the small mercy of not watching me process a normal sentence."
    "Traffic moves below the library in clean little intervals."
    "People cross at the light."
    "A cyclist glides past a parked delivery truck."
    "The world outside Sunday study appears to be continuing without asking for permission."
    m "You okay?"
    mc "Yeah."
    mc "Just thinking."
    m "About exams?"
    mc "Unfortunately, that would be more efficient."
    "She turns back."
    m "College?"
    "The word is careful."
    "Not a guess tossed across the table, but a card offered face-up."
    mc "A little."
    mc "Mom and I looked at the program Friday."
    mc "Then yesterday she managed to be normal about me studying with you, which was somehow more lethal than teasing me."
    m "She was normal?"
    mc "Devastatingly."
    m "I like your mom."
    mc "Most people with survival instincts do."
    "Monika folds her hands around her pen."
    m "Did the program look good?"
    "I answer before the easy joke arrives."
    mc "Yeah."
    "The word feels plain enough to show bone."
    mc "That was the problem."
    m "Because it became real?"
    mc "Basically."
    mc "A bad option is easy. You reject it and feel clever."
    mc "A good option starts making rooms look temporary."
    "Monika looks down at the review sheets between us."
    "This time, she does not correct the metaphor."
    m "I get that."
    "We sit in that for a second, the way you can only sit in something true with someone who won't poke it."
    "Then Monika squares the sticky notes again, which is her tell for steering the subject before it steers her."
    m "Can I say a thing that sounds intense for a Sunday?"
    mc "You've earned at least one."
    m "Whatever happens Monday, I'd rather walk in actually knowing it. Even if I bomb."
    m "The version of me that fakes it and gets away with it is somehow worse than the version that just...doesn't know."
    "She says it lightly. It isn't light."
    mc "That's a very you thing to believe."
    m "It's a very me thing to be annoying about, you mean."
    mc "Same sentence."
    "{i}She's not wrong, though.{/i}"
    "{i}There's a kind of pressure that makes the shortcut look reasonable. I can already feel the shape of it, a few days out.{/i}"
    "{i}The thought crosses my mind the way it crosses everyone's. I let it keep walking.{/i}"
    "A librarian stamps something at the desk across the room."
    "Soft thump."
    "Soft thump."
    "Time behaving administratively."
    m "For what it's worth..."
    "She stops."
    "Starts again."
    m "I'm glad you told me."
    mc "It wasn't exactly a strategic disclosure."
    m "Still."
    "She turns a sticky note over with her thumb."
    m "I like knowing what shape the hard thing is."
    "The sentence could be about studying."
    "It is not only about studying."
    mc "...Yeah."
    mc "Me too."
    "We let that sit."
    "Two whole breaths, maybe three."
    "Then Monika taps the science folder with the authority of someone saving us both from being too honest before lunch."
    m "Biology."
    mc "Cruel but effective subject change."
    "She holds up a card anyway."
    m "Which organelle modifies, sorts, and packages proteins for transport out of the cell?"
    mc "Golgi apparatus. The shipping department."
    m "Not mitochondria?"
    mc "Mitochondria make energy. They don't get to answer every question just because they're famous for it."
    m "Good."
    "She flips to the next card."
    m "Meiosis. Genetic variation among gametes."
    mc "Crossing over and independent assortment."
    m "Explain like you're not trying to win a sentence-length contest."
    mc "Chromosomes trade pieces, then line up in different combinations."
    mc "Different gametes, more variation."
    m "Nice."
    mc "Thank you."
    m "Ecology."
    m "What happens when a keystone species is removed?"
    mc "Cascade of changes across the community."
    m "Because?"
    mc "Because role matters more than size."
    mc "One piece can hold a whole arrangement together more than the arrangement wants to admit."
    "The line leaves my mouth and sits there."
    "Monika hears it."
    "I can tell because she does not immediately move to the next card."
    "I look at the table."
    mc "Ecology is rude."
    m "Very."
    "She writes cascade under a tiny drawing of a falling block tower."
    "The doodle is bad."
    "Lovingly bad."
    mc "Is that a tower?"
    m "It is an ecosystem."
    mc "That ecosystem has seen better days."
    m "It lost a keystone."
    "Her eyes meet mine, and for one second the joke becomes the container instead of the escape."
    "Then she switches folders, letting the moment go before it can ask anything of us."
    m "Chemistry."
    "And so we go down the list, the way you climb a staircase you've climbed before. Not gracefully. But without falling."
    "Le Chatelier shifts to relieve stress. A pH step is a factor of ten. The limiting reagent is whichever one runs out first and ruins everyone's afternoon."
    "She asks, I answer, she says {i}good{/i} in five slightly different tones, and somewhere in there I start being able to tell them apart."
    "The table stops looking like a study setup and starts looking like a shared excavation. Piles move. Answers become check marks. Small jokes turn into markers for places we might need to find again tomorrow."
    "Algebra arrives once the sunlight has shifted enough that Monika has to move her water bottle out of the glare."
    m "I saved algebra for after science because I'm kind."
    mc "Is that what this is?"
    m "You did bring the matrix sheet."
    mc "Against my better instincts."
    "Matrices multiply only when their inside numbers agree, and the order changes the answer, which feels personal coming from a branch of math usually too busy to be petty."
    "Domains forbid dividing by zero. Vertex form hides the vertex in plain sight, then flips a sign just to watch me flinch."
    "I get most of them. The ones I miss, I miss by hurrying, which Monika clocks without saying, because she's learned the exact shape of my impatience and decided not to feed it."
    "She checks the margin of her notes."
    "I know the look by now."
    "That is the president deciding whether she has permission to push one more thing."
    mc "Go ahead."
    m "I didn't say anything."
    mc "You didn't have to."
    "She laughs a little, but the push still arrives."
    m "English."
    mc "Already?"
    m "Last rotation before lunch."
    mc "Lunch has become the finish line."
    m "Then sprint responsibly."
    "She slides the English sheet over."
    "Present perfect for the things that started back then and had the nerve to keep going. {i}Since{/i} for the starting line, {i}for{/i} for the distance. Unreal past conditions for the regrets you get to conjugate instead of feel."
    mc "If I had studied more, I would have felt less nervous."
    "Monika pauses. Her mouth twitches."
    mc "Don't say painfully relevant sample sentence."
    m "Only internally."
    "She turns the page."
    m "Reading passage."
    m "A narrator wants an opportunity abroad but hesitates because people depend on him."
    "The library seems to lower its volume around that sentence."
    "Or I do."
    m "The question asks why he is hesitant."
    mc "He wants the opportunity but worries about leaving people who depend on him."
    m "Main idea with emotional friction."
    mc "Did you write that?"
    m "Maybe."
    mc "It is a good phrase."
    "She looks up."
    "Caught out."
    m "Thanks."
    "There is no joke ready."
    "I let that be true."
    "Her pen rests across the page."
    m "Do you think distance can be healthy?"
    "The question is too quiet to be only English practice."
    "I take a breath."
    mc "Yeah."
    mc "If it doesn't become disappearance."
    "Monika's eyes stay on mine."
    "Not demanding -- just there."
    m "That sounds like an answer."
    mc "It sounds like one I'd write if I had enough time to make it less incriminating."
    m "Maybe don't make it less incriminating."
    mc "On an English exam?"
    m "If the grammar is correct."
    "The laugh that gets out of me is smaller than usual, but it's still a laugh."
    "The window light reaches the edge of her folder."
    "For a second, the room is exactly what she asked for yesterday: low pressure about pressure, the two of us, a table, hard parts made smaller because someone stayed long enough to quiz them."
    "Then my stomach makes a noise loud enough to end the moment for us."
    m "Lunch?"
    mc "Lunch."
